    contentEn: `<h2>Getting from Bilbao Airport (BIO) to San Sebastián</h2>
<p>The most direct answer to "how do I get from Bilbao Airport to San Sebastián" is a private transfer: about 105 kilometres covered in roughly 1 hour and 20 minutes along the AP-8 motorway, door to door, with no changes and no waiting around for a shared shuttle to fill up. It's worth explaining why so many travellers land at Bilbao Airport (BIO) in the first place when their real destination is San Sebastián. San Sebastián does have its own small airport, EAS, but it operates a genuinely limited schedule of only a handful of flights a day to a short list of destinations, mostly with Volotea. Bilbao, by contrast, carries a far wider network of routes and airlines, including many more international connections, which makes it the more practical arrival point for the large majority of visitors heading to San Sebastián from outside Spain.</p>
<p>Once you land, a private driver is waiting inside the arrivals hall with a name board, already tracking your flight so a delayed landing never leaves you standing around wondering whether anyone is coming. From Loiu, the route runs east along the AP-8 coastal motorway, passing inland of Bermeo and Zarautz before dropping down into San Sebastián's built-up bay area. It's a comfortable, largely motorway drive rather than a winding coastal crawl, which is part of why the journey holds fairly steady at around 80 minutes regardless of the time of day, with only modest variation for weekend beach traffic in summer.</p>
<p>You can arrange your pickup point, flight number, and a fixed price in advance through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, and check live arrival information for your flight at <a href="https://www.aena.es">aena.es</a> before you travel. The same private service runs in reverse for your return journey, and if your trip also touches other parts of the Basque Country or northern Spain, our city-to-city transfers service can cover those legs too, with everything booked as a single, predictable itinerary rather than a string of separate bookings.</p>
<p>Families travelling with a child seat, groups needing a larger vehicle, and business travellers heading to meetings in San Sebastián should mention their requirements when booking, so the right car is waiting rather than being arranged on the spot. This matters more on a longer transfer like this one than on a short hop across town, since a vehicle mismatched to the group's size or luggage becomes a real inconvenience over an hour-plus drive. Our group event transfers service covers larger parties travelling together, while our corporate travel service is built around the tighter scheduling that business trips often require.</p>
<h3>About Bilbao Airport (BIO)</h3>
<p>Bilbao Airport (BIO) is a single-terminal airport in the municipality of Loiu, a short drive from Bilbao city centre. Its terminal building, designed by the Valencian architect Santiago Calatrava, is known locally by its nickname, La Paloma — "the Dove" — for the way its curved, wing-like roof appears to lift off the ground. As the Basque Country's busiest airport by a considerable margin, it's the natural gateway for travellers heading not just to Bilbao itself but across the wider region, San Sebastián very much included.</p>
<h2>About San Sebastián</h2>
<p>San Sebastián, known locally as Donostia, is routinely counted among Spain's most celebrated coastal cities, and it takes only one look at its setting to understand why. The city wraps around La Concha Bay, a near-perfect shell-shaped curve of sand framed by Monte Urgull to the east and Monte Igueldo to the west, with the small island of Santa Clara sitting almost exactly in the middle of the water. La Concha beach itself is frequently ranked among the finest urban beaches anywhere in Europe, and the elegant promenade running its full length, lined with wrought-iron railings and Belle Époque buildings, is as much a part of everyday Donostia life as the sand and water are.</p>
<h3>Pintxos and the Old Town</h3>
<p>Beneath Monte Urgull sits the Parte Vieja, San Sebastián's old town, where the city's food culture is on fullest display. Its narrow streets are dense with pintxo bars, the Basque take on tapas, where the custom is to hop from one bar to the next, sampling one or two small plates with a glass of txakoli or local wine at each stop rather than settling into a single sit-down meal. San Sebastián's reputation as one of Europe's leading food destinations reaches well beyond these bars into some of the most decorated fine-dining kitchens in the country, and the old town's dense, walkable layout means much of that experience is accessible on foot within a few blocks of the harbour, where the fishing fleet still works alongside the old town's church of Santa María.</p>
<h3>The Film Festival and the Wider City</h3>
<p>Each September, San Sebastián hosts the San Sebastián International Film Festival, one of the longest-running film festivals in the world and a genuine fixture on the international festival calendar, which noticeably changes the city's rhythm and its hotel availability for the duration of the event. Outside festival season, the pace is calmer and more coastal: across the Urumea river, the Gros district has its own beach, Zurriola, which faces the open Atlantic and draws surfers year-round, offering a distinctly different atmosphere from La Concha's sheltered, calmer waters just minutes away. A funicular ride up Monte Igueldo, or a walk along the paths of Monte Urgull past the old citadel, gives newcomers an easy, car-free sense of the city's layout within their first day.</p>
<h3>A City Shaped by Royal Summer Holidays</h3>
<p>Much of San Sebastián's elegant, Belle Époque character traces back to the late nineteenth century, when Queen María Cristina made the city her regular summer residence, drawing the Spanish court and European high society to the bay each year and cementing San Sebastián's reputation as a fashionable coastal retreat. The Miramar Palace, built for the royal family on a promontory overlooking La Concha, still stands today as one of the clearest physical reminders of that era, its gardens open to the public and offering one of the best vantage points over the whole bay. That period of royal patronage is also why the seafront carries such a consistent, grand architectural style, since much of it was built specifically to serve the aristocratic visitors the queen's presence attracted. Travellers interested in this history can also visit the San Telmo Museum, housed partly in a former Dominican convent at the edge of the old town, which covers Basque history, art, and society in more depth than a first walk through the Parte Vieja alone would reveal.</p>
<h2>Planning Your Transfer</h2>
<p>Whether your visit to San Sebastián is a short city break or the start of a longer trip around the Basque Country, arranging your Bilbao Airport transfer in advance means one less thing to think about on arrival. See our <a href="/bilbao-airport-transfer/">Bilbao Airport transfer</a> page for general details on arriving at BIO, our <a href="/san-sebastian/">San Sebastián</a> page for more on the city itself, and our airport transfers service if your itinerary includes other airports in the region.</p>`,
